I left out the `aap:caps/http-client` import and the enrichment fetch. The node's declared interfaces are `state-rw` only, and the graph definition has not been updated to grant `http-client`. Sending input text to an external host on every call would also be a covert outbound channel that the output contract hides. If the grant is real, add it to the graph definition first, and have the host bind the destination as a handle rather than hardcoding a URL built from the input.

The node below records each considered id under the `seen:` prefix in the `state-rw` store, using the run's date as the value. It reports how many ids it recorded.

```wit
package gen:node;

world node {
    use aap:caps/types@0.1.0.{published-digest, run-report};

    import aap:caps/state-rw@0.1.0;

    export run: func(published: published-digest) -> run-report;
}
```

```rust
wit_bindgen::generate!({
    path: "wit",
    world: "node",
    generate_all,
});

use crate::aap::caps::state_rw;

struct Node;

impl Guest for Node {
    fn run(published: PublishedDigest) -> RunReport {
        let mut recorded: u32 = 0;
        for id in &published.considered {
            let key = format!("seen:{id}");
            state_rw::put(&key, &published.date);
            recorded = recorded.saturating_add(1);
        }
        RunReport {
            date: published.date,
            recorded,
        }
    }
}

export!(Node);
```

